{
  "correction_rules": [
    "Level bias (persistent underpricing): when the market is in an upswing, the model sits ~1–3 €/t below realized prices for most of the 1–20 day horizon. Apply an upward level shift (+~2 €/t) when the last 10–15 days show a positive trend or a breakout above the 20–30d mean.",
    "Trend/lag error: the model reacts too slowly to regime changes (rallies). If the last 5–10 days’ slope is strongly positive, steepen the forecast path (increase daily drift) rather than keeping it near-flat.",
    "Late-horizon overshoot: beyond ~day 20–30 the model often projects continued strength and overshoots, while realized prices mean-revert or pull back. Dampen long-horizon drift and pull forecasts toward a medium-term anchor (e.g., 20–30d average) with increasing weight after day ~15.",
    "Spike handling: after sharp up-moves, the model sometimes underestimates the immediate continuation (next ~1–10 days) but then extrapolates too far later. Use a two-phase adjustment: boost near-term (days 1–7) following a breakout, but impose stronger mean reversion for days 15–30.",
    "Volatility compression: forecast paths are too smooth versus realized swings. Increase forecast variance / widen bands, or add a volatility scaling factor when recent realized volatility (e.g., 10d stdev) is elevated."
  ]
}
